Bear case

The technology works, but the investment does not.

Google successfully connects more products, devices and agents, but users do not pay enough for the extra utility.

Search economics weaken.

Interoperability becomes commoditized.

Other assistants own important interfaces.

Regulation limits context integration.

CapEx remains enormous.

Free cash flow fails to normalize enough to justify today's valuation.

This is a very important scenario because it would mean I was right about the technology and wrong about the stock.

That distinction belongs in the postmortem.

I assign this roughly 25%.

Base case

Alphabet becomes one of several dominant global AI platforms.

Gemini remains competitive. It does not need to be permanently number one.

Search stays economically resilient.

Cloud keeps scaling.

Personal context becomes useful enough to improve retention and product quality.

Cross-device continuity becomes real.

Agent infrastructure becomes a meaningful part of enterprise deployment.

CapEx stays high for a while but is eventually absorbed by much larger utilization and cash generation.

Alphabet wins because it controls enough context, infrastructure, distribution and execution to remain one of the central platforms in interconnected AI.

I assign this roughly 50%.

Bull case

The pieces actually become a coherent network.

Gemini becomes persistent across devices and software.

Search, Gmail, Maps, YouTube, Chrome, Android and Workspace become much more useful because context can move intelligently between them.

A2A and Agent Registry make Google Cloud an important control plane for agent ecosystems.

Watches and glasses make the system more persistent and more aware of real-world context.

Robotics turns physical action into another surface of the same stack.

At that point Alphabet is no longer well described as:

Search + YouTube + Cloud + Android + Devices + Other Bets.

The edges between the businesses become part of the moat.

That is where I think nonlinear upside could appear.

I assign this roughly 25%.

Final reasoning

I am not investing in Alphabet because I think Gemini will permanently be the best model.

That would be a much weaker thesis.

I am investing because I think AI is moving from isolated intelligence toward persistent, interconnected intelligence.

As that happens, the scarce layer shifts toward context, state, identity, permissions, coordination and action.

Alphabet already owns an unusually large fraction of the infrastructure needed to build that layer.

Search, Gmail, Maps, YouTube, Photos and Workspace can provide context.

Google Account can provide persistent identity.

Android and Chrome provide distribution.

Gemini provides reasoning.

A2A and Agent Registry provide pieces of interoperability, discovery and governance.

Google Cloud provides compute and enterprise deployment.

Phones, watches, cars and glasses provide different interfaces to the same user and eventually to the physical environment.

Gemini Robotics gives Alphabet an option on physical action.

The individual products are not the thesis.

The graph between them is the thesis.

The part I think is especially interesting is that several of the most important future nodes can look almost irrelevant when judged on current standalone revenue.

A watch can be financially small and still matter if it makes the whole AI layer more persistent.

Glasses can be financially small and still matter if they give the system environmental context.

Agent Registry can be financially small and still matter if it makes Google Cloud a control plane for agents.

Robotics can be financially small today and still give Alphabet a valuable option on embodied AI.

That is the pattern I want to recognize earlier:

assets that are too small to matter in today's segment model, but too strategically coherent and increasingly interconnected to treat as isolated experiments.

Alphabet is already a huge ecosystem. That part is obvious.

My bet is narrower:

the ecosystem is becoming an intelligence system.

If I am right, the value will come less from any one product and more from the fact that context, identity, reasoning and action can increasingly move through the entire graph.

At the current valuation I do not think this is a low-risk trade.

It is a long-duration position with real capital-intensity and valuation risk.

That is why I am putting 5%, not 10%, of the reference portfolio into it.

